Here’s what was added.

### Approach

Next.js recommends **`connection()`** from `next/server` when you need request-time rendering but you’re **not** using cookies, headers, or other request APIs. It waits for a real request so work below it is **not** prerendered at build time—exactly the `new Date()` / per-load timestamp case ([connection docs](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/connection) in your installed copy under `node_modules/next/dist/docs/.../connection.md`).

### Code

**`app/server-timestamp.tsx`** — async server component that calls `await connection()` then reads `new Date()`:

```1:21:/vercel/sandbox/app/server-timestamp.tsx
import { connection } from 'next/server'

/**
 * Renders the current server time on each request. Uses `connection()` so this
 * segment is not prerendered at build time (see Next.js docs: connection API).
 */
export default async function ServerTimestamp() {
  await connection()

  const now = new Date()
  const iso = now.toISOString()

  return (
    <p>
      Server timestamp:{' '}
      <time dateTime={iso}>{iso}</time>
    </p>
  )
}
```

**`app/page.tsx`** — renders that component (still a server component by default).

`npm run build` reports `/` as **Dynamic** (server-rendered on demand), not static prerender.

If you want the document title/metadata to match the new page, say so and we can align `app/layout.tsx` with it.

